I never said Gen 3 is as fast as Gen 4. I said there's no games out today that will leverage Gen 4 in any meaningful way. I said games in a couple years will and upgrading to get Gen 4 should be put on hold until then, you would also get DDR5 by waiting. If you have a 2 year old or newer flagship processor waiting right now is better. Anything older or lower in the CPU hierarchy then upgrading to a Ryzen platform and getting PCI-E Gen 4 now is a great choice while pairing it with a 30 series NVidia card or Big Navi if it is as big as people hope. If you have a 2 year old or newer flagship CPU and upgrade the GPU now you could upgrade everything but the GPU when the new CPU's come out in 2022 and get a noticeable Gen 4 bandwidth upgrade before the new GPU's come out so you don't have to wait for the GPU's to upgrade the CPU.

What I was saying has been proven with each generation of PCI-E. Early adoption before software leverages the greater bandwidth makes little difference. Difference scales that can be seen between different motherboards, ambient room temperatures, case airflow, and many other factors regardless if it is Intel or AMD or the same exact CPU/GPU. In a couple years just like all the other generation changes, there will be software leveraging the higher bandwidth and sticking a newer generation card into an older generation motherboard will be a significant bottleneck. That is when it will be important, today? It isn't.

